## Why This Is Asked
This question tests practical integration between AWS streaming services and Snowflake, evaluating real-world experience with real-time analytics, schema evolution, data masking, and system reliability—all critical skills in fintech data engineering.

## Key Concepts
- Snowpipe auto-ingest from S3 for real-time loading
- Parquet format with daily partitioning strategy
- AWS Glue Schema Registry for evolving schemas
- Snowflake dynamic data masking for PII protection
- End-to-end testing and comprehensive monitoring

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudocode: validate Snowpipe ingestion
const validateIngestion = async () => {
  // Test data completeness
  const sourceCount = await kinesis.getRecordCount();
  const snowflakeCount = await snowflake.query('SELECT COUNT(*) FROM transactions');

  // Validate schema consistency
  const schemaValidation = await validateSchema(sourceSchema, targetSchema);

  // Test recovery scenarios
  await simulateFailure();
  await verifyRecovery();
};
```

## Testing Strategy
1. **Ingestion Testing**: Validate record counts match between source and target
2. **Correctness Testing**: Compare sample records for data integrity
3. **Recovery Testing**: Simulate partial failures and verify Snowpipe's retry mechanism

## Why This Is Asked
Evaluates practical, beginner-appropriate streaming ingestion using common AWS data tooling and basic quality controls.

## Key Concepts
- Kinesis Data Streams for real-time data ingestion
- AWS Glue ETL and Data Catalog for data processing and metadata management
- Parquet format with date-based partitioning for optimized storage and queries
- Simple schema evolution strategies for handling changing data structures
- Data quality checks (required fields, types, nulls)
- CloudWatch alarms and end-to-end testing

## Code Example
